 um for machine learning and  then finally we have to build  kind of some sort of classification  algorithm potentially a regression  um model that will uh kind of identify  um you know a relationship hopefully  uh between our training data  and the labels um so  i think that you know everybody probably  here with us today knows the fun part of  this is  the classification part and maybe your  second  your second runner-up choice of the most  fun part maybe is future  engineering future transformation the  least fun uh  and kind of least exciting part of this  is  that labeled training data part people  uh don't enjoy labeling data um this  is you know it's a slow um a painful  process uh  fortunately you know i think during the  age of abundance  what we saw was this explosion in uh  companies that were offering uh that are  offering  their services to assist in data  labeling  and there actually are a ton of these  companies more than i expected when i  started looking  you know for names and suggestions there  are many many companies that offer  these resources but what i would suggest  is that it's actually better to consider  spending in-house spending time in-house  to develop your own label data  and i think of this phrase from uh  candide  um the book by voltaire  right so we need to tender our own  garden we need to tend to our own  uh harvest um so  you know getting labeled data i think uh  the reason why we would want to cultiva  cultivate our own garden um we we have  seen  kind of this explosion and commercial  providers who do  data annotation data labeling but it can  be quite expensive so  you know just for maybe a hundred  thousand um labeled samples  you could be paying between twenty five  thousand seventy five thousand us  dollars  um and you know honestly as a machine  learning practitioner  you probably already know a hundred  thousand samples may not be  sufficient to train a robust model  especially depending on how  high dimensional and complex your data  set is  and so that's really quite expensive for  a very small data set  another thing to kind of think about is  what's actually happening  at these companies where data is being  labeled there was  this sort of i think shocking article uh  many people found this  article shocking when it came out in  2018  um this alana samuels article the  internet is enabling a new kind of  poorly paid hell  which exposed that these  these services these data labeling  services are actually just employing  very low paid  workers to perform the annotation  and so as you might expect  when you have uh you know non-uh domain  experts  uh labeling data it's likely that the  quality of labels that you're getting  you know especially for  more domain specific data are not gonna  be very effective  um and when workers are being paid very  very low wages to do this kind of  labeling  as you might expect the quality of the  labels are going to vary and so  um you know this is one place where  you might think that you're spending uh  less money by outsourcing this work  outside of your company um but that  outsourcing work actually  can end up becoming a big expenditure if  you have to then  end up re-labeling your data in-house or  abandoning the project altogether  when you see that there's no signal  attached to the labels that you've been  provided  from this external service so  consider labeling your own data um  here's another place where i think we  tend to  end up spending a lot more money and  resources than we  intend to is this question of well which  model  should i use to build um build  this prototype um and you know i'm not  going to pick on any  model type uh here um but what i will  say is that  i have observed in my career of doing  applied machine learning that  people tend to make choices about models  in a very emotional  way right so they they frequently select  models that they have some emotional  attachment to because it's a model that  has been successful for them in the past  on prior projects  um or because it's a model that's kind  of attached to  some sort of glamour um or kind of  exciting  trend um what i what i think is that we  end up spending a lot more  money when we make those uh  model selection decisions in an  emotional way rather than  a scientific way and you know as data  scientists that's sort of where the  scientific  part comes in so you know what i would  say is think about the scientific  process when you're doing model  selection and for one thing  consider the context right so it's not  that a certain  model is going to be the best in all  cases probably  you know a a model might be the best for  this data set that you're working on or  for this problem space that you're  trying to  to solve for in this case and you can  actually test that hypothesis  by doing a comparison here's a  comparison  uh using the yellow brick library um  doing kind of three different  classification models on a single data  set to assess  um which of the models performs the best  and you know i think it's important to  point out that here we see  um a lot of variation um and we see some  cases where there might be some nuance  where  even if a model has a very high f1 score  if our concern really is in avoiding  false positives  um we may actually pick a model  uh kind of on on different on a  different basis  so you know what i'd say is you know do  model selection  um kind of intentionally and  scientifically rather than  um emotionally um you know so  rather than kind of doing what's on the  left-hand side of the screen just  googling what the best model is actually  run  experiments like what's on the right  hand side of the screen where  we select several models and then we use  the data and some kind of cross  validation  measure to systematically determine  the model that is best suited to this  data set  um pick simple models in the beginning  uh  and and try several in parallel identify  the ones that are the best of the ones  that are  are weakest and set an initial baseline  and let that  initial baseline help set the ground  for hyper parameter tuning which comes  next  um so speaking of hyperparameter tuning  this is another place where  we spend as a community of machine  learning practitioners we spend  a lot of time and a lot of resources  and so there are a number of different  ways to do hyper  parameter tuning whether you're kind of  using  the tools that are available in a  library like scikit-learn let's say in  doing something like randomized  search or grid search to using kind of a  bayesian search method  some sort of evolutionary method or  gradient based method  um or whether you are using a you know  quote-unquote auto ml  um you know this is a place where we  spend  a lot of money a lot of time  particularly if you're doing  what's called auto ml which is honestly  really just  usually a combination of the other  things on this list  but done in a black box fashion um  and uh you know where you have to pay a  lot  rather than being able to implement some  of these um  search methods um on your own  uh kind of with more intention about how  you're how you're executing that search  automl tends to be um the most expensive  of all of these uh just because you  can't really tell what's happening under  the hood  um so what i would say is that no matter  what type of hyper parameter tuning  methodology you're using remember  that search is very very difficult and  it gets more difficult  the higher dimensional your data set  becomes  there are many clever optimizations uh  that try to speed  up um the the search process but then  even with those optimizations there's no  guarantee that you'll find a solution  you'll there's no guarantee that you  know if you do find a solution that it's  the optimal solution  um and as your search space gets larger  larger so as your dimensions get bigger  as you have more data  more features and more instances  the amount of time that it takes to do  hyperparameter tuning  increases exponentially  so what to do instead um so if we're  thinking about  a more uh efficient way to do  a hyper perimeter tuning you might think  um  about honestly starting by just reducing  your feature space this can be a way to  dramatically reduce the cost of doing  hyperparameter tuning  what we're looking at here is a yellow  brick visualization  called the recursive feature elimination  visualizer and this wraps  the rfe cv method in scikit-learn and  what it allows us to do is identify  the um the set of features  in our data that is necessary to achieve  some threshold score whether that's your  f1 score or your coefficient  of determination you might also  consider thinking about your train  and test data thinking about how the  model changes  as you give your algorithm more data or  as you take data away  what we're looking at here are yellow  brick learning curve visualizations  for different types of algorithms for  different data sets and thinking about  um you know what happens as we give the  model a little bit more of our data for  training  do we see the training and  cross-validation scores converge  do does one drop while the other  increases  these are things that can help us make  decisions before we spend a lot of time  on hyper parameter tuning to kind of  reduce  that search space and make more  effective choices  um another thing that we might want to  do is really  think deeply about the parameter ranges  that we're searching in when we do  something like a grid search  um you know for for a grid search um  what we  what we were doing is we're setting  multiple different parameter ranges to  explore simultaneously  um but oftentimes before you do that  it's worth it to  explore them individually in a single  parameter range and understand  what that optimal range is to be  searching inside and  that's something that you can do with  this validation curve visualization  which is also from the yellow brick  library so how to do  uh tuning in a more economical uh way  uh well first of all only tune the best  performing models so don't  it's not worth it to do hyper parameter  tuning on all of the models  that you've explored only on the ones  that are best performing  if you can do what you can to reduce  your feature space  either using a future selection or some  kind of feature  engineering or dimensionality reduction  technique make sure you understand the  parameter ranges that you're searching  within and you're not doing it randomly  so you're really thinking about what  what is a sensible  range to be searching within move  towards adding complexity to  a model in a purposeful way so think  about  whether or not the error is coming from  variance or from  bias think about you know if the model  is clearly underfitting that's a good  case  for adding complexity if the error is  not converging that's another  good reason for adding complexity so  make sure you have a good reason for  for adding that complexity and when you  do add complexity do it gradually  and make sure that as you do add  complexity through your hyper parameters  that your train and test scores  are both increasing  another place where i am seeing a lot of  teams  expend a lot of resources in terms of  time and money  is with the development environments  that they choose to use  to do exploratory data analysis  and initial modeling attempts so  i'm seeing more and more teams use  things like  azure data bricks and amazon sage maker  as the initial starting point for  doing kind of an exploration of the  features in a data set  and looking at correlations between  features  and exploring initial models that might  be  a good fit for the data set but what i  would say  is that you know this is generally not  not really necessary so if you consider  that  those conveniences of being able to use  these cloud hosted  tools i i they do add convenience  uh certainly right you don't have to  worry about um configuring spark spark  locally you don't have to  worry about the python version that you  have installed  versus the one that your teammate has  installed that is convenient but  consider  whether or not that's really worth the  expenditure  during an initial prototyping phase  you pay a lot for that convenience in  fact you know every moment  that those notebooks are running in  those cloud-hosted tools you're paying  or your company is paying  those are all opportunities where if you  were just running those notebooks  locally  or exploring um you know the code  locally  in a local development environment  you could potentially be saving a  tremendous amount of money  um and i do hear you know there there  are people who this will say well i  can't do exploratory data analysis  on my local laptop because i'm working  with a massive data set  um and in those cases what i would say  is um  okay well have you tried done down  sampling uh  you know it's actually extremely cheap  to just take a very small random sample  of your data to perform some initial  exploratory analysis and what you might  find is that you can conduct a lot of  your preliminary experiments  locally before you move things to the  cloud and  you know i i certainly as a  applied machine learning practitioner i  understand that you know at some point  we often do have to move everything to  the cloud so that we can kind of have  our  code running as microservices in a  you know robust scalable way  but you know that initial prototyping  step uh oftentimes can happen locally  um consider also that um  you know some of the tools that we're  using  um lend themselves to  bad habits they give us very limited  memory there's a quote by george  santayana  that those who cannot remember the past  are condemned to repeat it  you know for those of us who do  exploration  in machine learning i think a lot of  times that  condemnation manifests  something like this where you'll be  working inside a jupiter notebook  you'll be exploring some models uh  you've been running your notebook for  a while um you have uh done a tremendous  amount of  feature engineering and exploration and  at some point something goes wrong with  the notebook and you're unable to  recover some of your data so whether or  not that means you've lost  the model or the progress of the model  if something crashed part way through  if you lost kind of your intermediate  future  engineering components whether you lost  the final model  consider that all of the time that goes  into that development  is has a cost and there is  a cost aside from just being embarrassed  at the end  there's a cost that's associated with  losing  losing data so one thing that i would  suggest and this is something that you  can do kind of in the context of  whatever your ide is  whether it's hosted whether it's local  whether it's a jupiter notebook or  python scripts or something else  get into the habit of serializing  everything  so make sure there are checkpoints in  your code where you're serializing not  just the model but any  engineered features so that could be  future vectors it could be embedding  spaces  store the stop words that you've  identified as being really  um meaningful uh in your context for  removing  things and and making sure the important  words  bubble to the top you can serialize any  lexicons that you've developed serialize  the scores of your models right so not  just  the pickle let's say but also  the scores that are associated with that  saved model  you can also save all of the diagnostic  plots that you've done  using seabourn or matplotlib or  yellowbrick  you can save those diagnostic plots help  you remember  how the model that you're saving was  performing  the other big thing that i see a team  sort of forget to do is to  to serialize and track the training time  so if you end up with two models that  perform  approximately the same one 